# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
#
# Drop the EFI RTC driver so the PMIC RTC (rtc-pm8xxx) owns /dev/rtc0.
#
# The arm64 defconfig ships CONFIG_RTC_DRV_EFI=y.  On nabu that driver
# registers first (built-in, 0.414s), wins the name "rtc0" -- the name the
# kernel's hctosys looks up (CONFIG_RTC_HCTOSYS_DEVICE="rtc0") -- and then
# fails every single read:
#
#     [0.414707] rtc-efi rtc-efi.0: hctosys: unable to read the hardware clock
#     /sys/class/rtc/rtc0/date  ->  Input/output error
#
# Three things follow from that, and they are the reason this fragment exists:
#
#   1. The kernel never loads the hardware clock at boot: rtc_hctosys() runs
#      when the device named rtc0 registers (drivers/rtc/class.c), it reads
#      rtc-efi, the read fails, and the system starts on the firmware clock.
#   2. timedatectl is unusable: systemd's clock_getrtc() opens /dev/rtc0 and
#      gets "Failed to read RTC: Input/output error" for every query.
#   3. The clock that does work, rtc-pm8xxx (CONFIG_RTC_DRV_PM8XXX=m), is
#      pushed to /dev/rtc1, where neither the kernel nor systemd looks.
#
# With the EFI driver compiled out, rtc-pm8xxx is the first RTC to register
# (~4.2s, when the module loads), so it becomes rtc0: hctosys then loads the
# system clock from it, and timedatectl reads it fine.
#
# The EFI RTC is broken on this platform anyway, so nothing is lost.  Same
# rationale applies to the production and production-7.2 products, whose
# defconfigs both set CONFIG_RTC_DRV_EFI=y.
#
# Requires the PMIC RTC to hold sane time: it is written once after NTP sync
# (see nabu-debian), and it is battery-backed so it keeps counting across
# reboots.
#
# CONFIG_RTC_DRV_EFI is not set
